The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (freeze_all_ptr && !(sb->s_type->fs_flags & FS_POWER_FREEZE))
    return;
Reason:
The condition was inverted: it returned early (skipped freezing) when `freeze_all_ptr` was true (indicating a “freeze all” request) and the filesystem lacked `FS_POWER_FREEZE`.  The correct logic should skip freezing only when `freeze_all_ptr` is false (i.e., not a global freeze) and the filesystem does not have the `FS_POWER_FREEZE` flag.  The reversal caused **all** filesystems to be frozen even when `filesystem_freeze_enabled` was false, which led to the triggering of `WARN_ON_ONCE()` in `__set_task_frozen()`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!freeze_all_ptr && !(sb->s_type->fs_flags & FS_POWER_FREEZE))
    return;
Reason:
By adding the logical negation (`!freeze_all_ptr`), the early return now correctly occurs only when there is **no** “freeze all” directive **and** the filesystem lacks `FS_POWER_FREEZE`.  This restores the intended behavior: filesystems are frozen only if either a global freeze is requested or they have the appropriate flag, eliminating the spurious freezing of all file systems and the associated warnings.

